course: dictionary
lesson: 13857
title: Judicial: words about judges and courts
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words belong to a special world, like law. Judicial is one of them. Look at the clues around it. «The judicial system decides who is guilty and who is innocent.» The words guilty and innocent tell you this is about a court, so judicial must have something to do with judges and courts.
explain: «The judge made a judicial decision after hearing both sides.» A decision is a choice. Here a judge made the choice in a court, so judicial means done by a court or a judge. Try swapping in your own meaning: "the court's decision" fits the sentence, so you are on the right track.
explain: In citizenship lessons you learn that a country has three branches of government: the lawmakers who make laws, the people who run the country, and the judicial branch. The judicial branch is made up of courts, and it is kept separate from the other two.
fact: Judicial means to do with judges, law courts and the way the law is applied.
fact: «Judicial» can describe a decision made by a court or a judge.
fact: The judicial branch of government is made up of courts, separate from the lawmakers and the government.
fact: You may also see the phrases «judicial review» and «judicial system».
exercise: «The judicial system protects everyone in the country.» What does judicial mean here? | To do with the weather | *To do with judges, courts and the law | To do with sports teams | Cooking and baking
exercise: «The court made a judicial decision about the case.» What does a judicial decision mean? | A guess about the weather | A party for friends | A choice made in a shop | *A choice made by a judge or a court
exercise: In a country's government, which branch is made up of courts? | Sporting | Executive | Legislative | *Judicial
test: «The judicial branch is separate from the lawmakers.» What is the judicial branch made up of? | Lawmakers | Shops | *Courts | Schools
test: Which word fits best? «The ___ review checked whether the new law was fair.» | Musical | Hungry | Sunny | *Judicial
exam: Which meaning best fits judicial? | *To do with judges, courts and the law | To do with flowers and gardens | To do with loud music | To do with ships at sea
